1. What is the difference between a process and a thread?
Hmm... A process is like a big box containing everything it needs: memory, code, data, etc. A thread is like a smaller path inside that box, which executes instructions.
Process is an independent unit of execution. Has its own memory.
Thread is a lightweight subpart of a process. It shares the memory with other threads of the same process.
Example: Running Google Chrome = process
Each tab or script running inside = thread


✅ 2. Why are threads called lightweight processes?
Let me think... Threads are called lightweight processes because:
They don’t need separate memory space — they just use what the process already has.
Creating and switching between threads is much faster and less resource-heavy than doing the same for full processes.
So yeah, they're like “mini-processes” running under the same roof.


✅ 3. What are the benefits of multithreading?
Hmm, the main benefits are:
Better CPU usage – Threads can run in parallel on multi-core CPUs.
Responsiveness – UI won't freeze, because background tasks run on different threads.
Faster execution – Tasks are split and handled independently.
Memory efficiency – All threads share the same memory.
Example: While typing in MS Word:
One thread handles typing,
Another checks spelling,
Another auto-saves.


✅ 4. What are user-level vs kernel-level threads?
Yeah so…
Feature	User-Level Thread (ULT)	Kernel-Level Thread (KLT)
Managed by	User library	OS Kernel
Speed	Fast to create/switch	Slower
Kernel-aware?	No	Yes
If 1 thread blocks?	All block	Only that one blocks
Used when	Speed is priority	Need true parallelism



✅ 5. Explain context switching between threads and processes.
Ahh okay, so:
Context switching means saving the current state (registers, memory info) and loading another’s.
Between processes → heavier: OS switches full memory context, stack, etc.
Between threads → lighter: Memory is shared, only thread-specific data (like program counter, stack) is switched.
So basically, thread switching is faster and more efficient.


✅ 6. What are the types of multithreading models?
There are mainly 3 models:
Many-to-One – Many user threads map to one kernel thread
Simple, but not scalable (one blocks, all block)
One-to-One – Each user thread maps to one kernel thread
Parallelism possible, but costly for many threads
Many-to-Many – Many user threads map to many kernel threads\
Most flexible and scalable


✅ 7. Can threads in the same process access each other’s data?
Yes! That's actually one of the core features of threads.
All threads of a process share memory, so they can access the same data structures.
But... it can cause race conditions or bugs if not synchronized properly (like two threads writing at same time).


✅ 8. What happens if one thread crashes?
Hmm... if one thread crashes:
It can affect the whole process, because all threads share the same memory space.
For example, if one thread corrupts a shared variable or crashes the heap, all threads might go down.
That’s why proper error handling and synchronization is super important.


✅ 9. How is scheduling handled between multiple threads?
Oh right! So the Operating System’s scheduler handles this — especially in the case of kernel-level threads.
Each thread gets a time slice or priority.
Threads can be preempted (paused and resumed later).
In user-level threads, the thread library handles scheduling, not the OS.
So depending on the type (user/kernel), either the thread library or the OS handles the scheduling.